Date: Tue, 2 Mar 93 20:48:59 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: POLI: Congressional Chaplains

It appears to me as if you are approaching the morality of taxation from a
viewpoint so far different from my own that our discussion will generate
more heat than light.  I'll doggedly make a few more comments, though, and
then let the matter drop.

>However the part you didn't carry over in your reply was that there are
>stipulations in the law that prohibit churches from certain activities
>in order to *earn* that exemption. Those churches which enjoy their exemption,
>but disregard the prohibitions should be held accountable in my view.
>Do you see it otherwise?

Yes and no.  I see a great benefit in having a uniform set of laws applied
equally to all parties.  The rule of law demands as much.  But what if the
law being equally applied is an unjust one?  A morally abhorrent law is
hardly redeemed by affecting everyone with equal injustice.  You say
churches *earn* their exemptions.  I say they we all deserve tax
exemptions--not because we've earned them, but because noone else has a
claim to the fruits of our labors.

I conclude that we share the same view of the rule of law, but a different
view about the morality of taxation.

>how about the escapee that does so by climbing over your back, preventing you
>from doing so yourself, I suppose you'd salute his superior wit and skill
>despite the potential for your own imminent punishment!

That would be a different scenario, and not applicable to the case at hand.
 Those who escape taxation do not coerce me or prevent me from seeking my
own exemptions.

> Or how about the added burden placed on the remaining  prisoners due to the
>escape.

Those who escape taxation are not responsible for any additional burden
statists place on me.  I have no right to enslave others to relieve my own
burdens.  I must fight to free myself.

>Simply put Tom, nobodys holding a gun to your head to stay, or to remain in 
>the system. 

This appears to be a variation of the "American:  Love it or leave it!"
argument.  Anyone who posts to this list should be now be familiar with its
refutation, so I'll save my keystrokes for better projects.

>Last week I had an interesting encounter in the local market . . .

I really enjoyed this story!  Regardless of our different views about
political theory, Owen, I can see that we advocate the same approach to the
practice of supermarket political practices!

>LUX ./. owen


