> person would cheer the criminals' efforts to recapture the escapee?  The
> appropriate response is to salute the his good fortune and to redouble your
> efforts to throw off your own shackles.

I can't see this as an apt analogy in this case.  The churches are
hardly freedom-loving anti-statists boldly, or cleverly, or even just
luckily escaping from the tax burden imposed on the rest of us.

I think a more apt analogy is:  Imagine that you live in a neighborhood
dominated by a criminal gang that extorts protection money from you and
all your neighbors.  (Imagine?)  But there are a few who for one reason
or another are in the gang's good graces--many of them even work very
hard at sucking up to the gang in order to retain that status--and these
the gang doesn't rob.  Do you really feel a warm glow in your heart when
you see that they are free of the burden that you can't escape?

