Date: Tue, 2 Mar 93 18:12:54 GMT
From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

An issue which I haven't seen addressed in this thread is that asymptotic
growth is a rather strange goal.

If you leave your entire stake in the game on every round (whether you
actually bet it all or hold back a fraction to hedge against big losses),
and go on playing indefinitely, it doesn't matter what the return is (at
least, so long as it is never so negative as to require you to put more
money in).  For the game to actually be useful, you have to take money out
of it from time to time in order to use.  Money is only useful when someone
actually spends it.

How is the analysis of these and other stock-market games affected if you
don't maximise asymptotic growth, but instead maximise a constant income
which you take out of the game on each turn?  Or an exponentially growing
income, to finance an ever more expansive and expensive lifestyle?

