Date: Tue, 2 Mar 93 21:47:21 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: POLI: Congressional Chaplains

>I can't see this as an apt analogy in this case.  The churches are
>hardly freedom-loving anti-statists boldly, or cleverly, or even just
>luckily escaping from the tax burden imposed on the rest of us.

I'm not a religious man, but I'm willing to give at least some churches
great credit for opposing statism.  Consider that the struggles between
Church and State in early medieval Europe from about 1050 to 1200 helped to
support a thriving polycentric legal system.  Or consider the central role
that religious arguments played in supporting the American colonials' call
for revolution.  Think about the integral relations between churches and
the abolitionist movement and, later, the civil rights movement.  Many
religious leaders opposed the Vietnam war, as well.

I'm not saying that churches consistently oppose statism, of course.  But
so long as they are not actively supporting statist mechanisms I am happy
to see them establish harbors from the rising gale of institutional
coercion--even if I am personally unwilling to take shelter in them.  I'll
even excuse religions that praise statism, so long as they don't seize it
as a tool to use against me.  In fact, one reason I am so adamant about
maintaining the barrier between Church and State is because religious
statism is a doubly deadly doctrine.  If churches are taxed, they will no
doubt begin to demand greater say in government operations.

(BTW, Owen would have had a great deal more success convincing me of his
views had he stressed that the religious right is using its exemptions to
gain political power.  Instead, he appeared to focus on merely the inequity
of their escaping taxes while he paid his.)


