HEADLINE: The shape of things to come: National Review asked five 
distinguished conservatives to muse about what the future may hold - 
politically, socially, economically, technologically - as we approach the 
end of a century and a millennium

BYLINE: Stelzer, Irwin M. ; Gilder, George ; Macrae, Norman ; 
Wanniski, Jude ; Murray, Charles

 BODY:

AMERICANS appear to have made two fundamental decisions about 
their future. One should depress conservatives, the other cheer them. 

We seem to have ignored conservative wisdom and, instead, left social 
policy to the bad guys. Welfare schemes that guarantee a permanently 
dependent underclass are now thoroughly embedded in our political 
and social life. Neither the intellect of Charles Murray nor the energy 
of Jack Kemp can cope with the grim determination of ghetto 
politicians to hold their constituents in thrall by denying them 
incentives to work and to establish normal family lives; by insulating 
them from pressures to use our language and otherwise enter the 
American mainstream; and by blaming the lethal nature of street life 
on the police rather than on the dealers and muggers. 

These malignancies, and others Uke them, might, of course, be excised 
from the body politic. But that would mean enduring short-term pain 
in exchange for long-term gain-something our political system is not 
organized to do. There is simply no constituency for it. The apparent 
beneficiaries of the welfare system (in fact, its victims) can't do without 
their transfer-payment "fix"; the dispensers of the welfare narcotic, 
fearing redundancy if those clients kick the habit, dole out enough 
money to retain control; the great majority crinkles up its nose, moves 
to the suburbs, and contents itself with a tax revolt that is negated by 
huge budget deficits. 

The good news is that a country as vast and diverse as ours takes a lot 
of killing. And, because of another, happier choice, it is likely to thrive, 
although the benefits of that prosperity may be denied to a minority by 
that group's putative benefactors. 

This happy choice has been to stick with a free-market economy, to 
reject the Reich-Kuttner-Dukakis plea for a centrally managed 
economy, the Gephardt drive for a gutting of the world's free trading 
system, and the populist move (lately abetted by Treasury Secretary 
Nicholas Brady) to turn the Federal Reserve system into an engine of 
permanent inflation. True, we do not always walk the straight and 
narrow: we protect this or that industry, subsidize mostly rich farmers 
at the expense of consumers, bail out Chrysler, and institutionalize 
sloth by refusing to privatize the postal system. But basically we hold 
to the notion that markets provide surer and more enduring cures for 
our ills than do bureaucrats. As a result, the crises of the late 1980s and 
early 1990s will soon be dim memories. 

Consider, first, the so-called Third World debt crisis and the talk of the 
collapse of Latin American economies. Fortunately, the U.S. banks 
were unable to persuade a market-oriented Reagan Administration to 
bail them and their debtors out. The result in Latin America has been 
privatization to relieve the drain on resources created by overmanned 
state monopolies, removal of trade barriers so that imports can 
compete with overpriced domestic goods, and a generation of 
politicians beginning to see that markets, not ministers, can deliver 
prosperity. It all bodes well. 

Then there is the related banking crisis. Billions in loans to Third 
World countries, and a wave of lending to energy companies 
prospecting for $ 100-a-barrel oil and real-estate companies eager to 
build empty office buildings and shopping malls, laid the banks low. 
Write-offs soared and profits plunged. 

Again, we had a Reagan policy of, Don't do something, just stand 
there," to thank for a solution. Banks wrote down loans. Shareholders 
lost money. Prices of bank stocks fell-until they reached a level that 
attracted new capital into the system. And bureaucracies were 
reduced, to the point where 87 per cent of America's 12,340 commercial 
banks turned a profit last year. 

So, too, with the trade deficit.  Freely fluctuating exchange rates, the 
bane of economic planners, brought the dollar to levels that 
contributed to an export boom. The trade deficit-centrists' standard 
excuse for subsidizing some industries and technologies, and 
protecting others-is withering away. 

That should put paid to the argument that America is becoming non-
competitive, a manufacturing giant shriveling into a nation of 
hamburger flippers. In fact, the nation's factories now account for 23.2 
per cent of America's GNP, up from the 20 per cent postwar low in 
1982 and equal to the level of output in the 1960s when American 
industrial prowess was taken for granted. 

Since most people are more pragmatic than ideological, and since 
conservative economic policies work, they should continue to sweep 
the field. 

George Gilder 

In Recent months, conservatives have been celebrating the feats of 
computer technology in war.  In coming years, however, we will be 
able to use computer technology to blow away our enemies in the 
media and the schools as effectively as in the Persian Gulf. A cautious 
projection of current trends suggests that, within a decade or so, many 
of the multi-million-dollar computer powers displayed in the war will 
be available in home appliances costing a few hundred dollars. 
Compounding this gain in computers, the movement of fiber optics 
into homes and offices will bring a similar millionfold advance in 
communi cations. 

I am a conservative.  I thick secular culture is an irredeemable fiasco; 
estranged from God as the source of all truth and beauty, art rots to 
triviality and evil.  Nonetheless, I believe that this culture has 
produced on incomparable achievement, scaled a summit as beautiful 
and true as any Gothic cathedral. That pinnacle it its technology. 
Mostly based on the microchip-a weave of wires and switches as 
complex as a street map of America, covering a sliver of silicon the size 
of a thumbnail-this supreme technology is entirely a product of 
conservative values.  Yet most conservatives today still fail to benefit 
from it. Many of us send our children to secular schools that disdain 
our deepest values, leave students inept to compete in a global 
economy, waste classroom time on the "profound" ecological and 
religious "insights" of Sioux sages, and devotedly teach the digital 
skills of putting a condom on a banana. Some of us dispatch our 
teenagers to private schools, at some $ 20,000 per year, that cannot 
even save their students from sex, porn, and drugs. Many of us send, 
or hope to send, our children to universities such as Harvard and Yale, 
which are the festering heart of the surrounding darkness. At night, 
most of us watch news" programs and other television 
"entertainments" that are created by socialists and other levelers chiefly 
as propaganda for more government power. Or we watch movies full 
of sexual hedonism, violence, and evil, and disdainful of the ethic of 
work, family, and faith that makes all civilization possible. 

Playing to the prurient interests and morbid fears of mass audiences is 
the forte of liberal broadcasters everywhere. Conservatives make lousy 
panders. But the new computer and communications technology will 
render broadcasting obsolete. As in the current world of magazines 
and books, works will chiefly succeed by appealing to special interests 
and aspirations rather than

couch-potato cravings. Within the next decade it will be possible for all 
of us to have fully interactive "telecomputers" in our homes that can 
cheaply receive, process, and store digital video images from virtually 
any classroom, library, church, laboratory, museum, sports event, 
television station, theater, or database in the world. On high-resolution 
screens far superior to classrooms, theaters, and chalkboards, you will 
be able to summon the best teachers and professors, preachers and 
performers, doctors and writers in the world at any time you wish. 
Using the same computers, you will also be able cheaply to originate 
and transmit programs of your own. With millions of available 
channels rather than the current hundreds, you will be able to form 
markets and alliances with others who share your tastes and beliefs 
anywhere on the globe. 

By the very law of the microcosm from which it springs, computer 
power favors individuals against every top-down establishment and 
bureaucracy, from the centralized school and factory to the centralized 
nation-state. Just as multi-national corporations can now range the 
world for workers and capital-avoiding the taxes and regulations of 
inhospitable countries-in coming years you will be able to escape the 
constraints of time and place and media power in most of your cultural 
and economic life. 

Crucial to these gains, however, is unleashing the phone companies to 
install fiber optics in every home, as the phone company in Japan plans 
to do. Media opponents offer a seductive Luddism, sentimental 
statism, and TV communalism that appeal to some conservatives, who 
still see technological progress as a force of the Left. The key to the 
coming triumph of conservatism will be how vigorously conservatives 
support the mostly American technological forces that they made 
possible through their defense of the traditional culture of capitalism. 

Norman Macrae  

THE 1980s surge of radical conservatism-led by that improbable pair, 
Reagan and Thatcher-swelled from a sensible recognition which will 
keep recurring, namely, that it is hopelessly inefficient to let politicians 
spend so much of our money for us. Any factory in Omsk eventually 
becomes less than one-sixth as efficient per manhour and per ruble as 
any Western factory that changes all the time to compete in free 
markets. The realization is about to dawn that the services provided by 
public monopolies in the West operate at a similar under-one-sixth of 
potential productivity. This applies to education, crime prevention, 
environmental protection, welfare services, even currently admired 
defenses (far too expensive a steamroller half-crushed that little 
Saddam nut). The task of conservatives will be to keep introducing 
competition into all these services, imposing one main rule: If 
somebody introduces a better or cheaper way of tackling a problem, it 
must bring the innovator a lot of money.  At present, in public 
monopolies, it brings him his colleagues' fury and pique. 

The nastiest politicians spending too much of GNP (Communists, 
Third World dictators) spend most of it on their armies, their secret 
police, and themselves. The nicest (called liberals in America, social 
democrats in Europe and Japan) genuinely thought that spending by 
them would make things better for the poor. Instead, and inevitably, 
they have created a permanent drug-crippled, crime-crucified 
underclass. Although about 13 per cent of Americans are still below 
the poverty line, the best sociologists show that an American has under 
1 per cent danger of staying long in poverty provided he or she does 
three things: gets and stays married, completes high school, stays more 
than a year in his or her first-job even if at the minimum wage. Anti-
poverty policy must turn to incentives for such actions. 

America may face two dangers during this march to common sense. 
One is that Christianity, which in Europe is too much based on a pity 
ethic, might in America come to be too self-righteous. The other is 
America's absurd political system, which ensures a congressman re-
election if he arranges for a lot of federal money to be spent on his 
constituents: i.e., if he turns his constituency into an Omsk. 

Fortunately, North America's Third World hinterland (Latin America 
and probably the Caribbean) is entering a better patch, while Western 
Europe's hinterland enters a disappointing one. The two religions to 
the European Community's south and east (Islam and socialism) are 
fearfully unsuitable for modern times. Islam is at present the same 14-
plus centuries from the birth of its Prophet as Christianity was from 
the birth of Jesus at the time of Torquemada's Inquisition. It looks it. 

We Western Europeans have long known that Eastem European 
Communism would not outlast this century. The question was: Would 
it be replaced by a free-market system that could quickly make it six 
times richer, as West Germany and Japan became after 1945, or by an 
overnationalist and hyperinflationary mess like the one that crippled 
South America in its lost century after Bolivar? The ex-Comms are 
going post-Bolivar. Much blame lies on Helmut Kohl, the conservative 
dummy of the past decade. Kohl found it vote-winning to convert one 
D-Mark for one Ostmark, at a time when markets showed the Ostmark 
was worth only about one-sixth a D-Mark. He is now amazed that 
more and more East Germans become unemployed, because they have 
to be paid six times what their present production is worth. The rest of 
the EC thinks it will stabilize best if it anchors its currencies to the 
German Mark, which will be as stabilizing as being anchored to an 
electric eel. From Japan and its hinterland has come the most dramatic, 
but unnoticed, recent event. Japanese-run factories in (e.g.) Thailand 
now suddenly have as high a productivity and (more important) 
quality control as Japanese factories back home in Osaka, at maybe 
one-fifth of the labor costs. Manufacture of virtually all transportable 
and thus exportable goods will move to Third World countries, 
especially East Asia as vast India and China enter the mass of 
manufacturing mankind. Because they had few birth-control pills in 
the 1970s, Third World countries have a vast population of newly 
literate twenty-year-olds, all eager to work with their hands; while 
Japan, America, and Europe have fewer twenty-year-olds, most of 
them wanting to work with their brains. 

The logical split becomes: Third World manufacturing countries, First 
World brainworkers. That sets the First World a horrendous problem: 
Who does our menial jobs? Either a) our own uneducated youths (a 
host, thanks to failed public education) and less-educated grandpas, or 
b) more immigrants. Japan will pass its junk jobs to its grandpas; 
Europe and America will slip into more immigration. Japan (even 
though going into an America-like trade deficit) will prosper better, 
even if it does not deserve to. 

Jude Wanniski 

FIRST, to distinguish between "populist conservatives" and 
"conservative elitists," I associate the former with Jefferson, the latter 
with Hamilton. The former is more democratic, the latter more 
republican. Jefferson is associated with aspirations, the realization of 
unfulfilled potential; Hamilton is identified with the protection of 
already realized potential. Civilization improves itself by refining the 
mechanisms that balance these legitimate, competing interests. 

In the next phase of civilization's march, which should carry us well 
into the next century, conservative populists should work on the 
concept that the poor have to be allowed to become rich. That is, 
people naturally try to improve themselves, and if government does 
not interfere with their efforts, many will eventually succeed. This is 
not a libertarian formulation, for it does not necessarily argue for less 
government. There is plenty for government to do, as long as it does 
not actively suppress individual potential. 

All varieties of conservatives understand that government cannot 
make the poor rich in any meaningful way. It can, though, frustrate the 
efforts of the young, and of aspiring blacks, Hispanics, and other 
minorities. Inflation hurts these people the most, cheating them out of 
their savings as they try to put together money for school or to start a 
business. Taxing labor excessively damages capital. In the same way, 
the taxation of capital gains hurts most the people who have most of 
their gains in front of them, not the old or the wealthy, who have most 
of their gains behind them. Government must allow the poor to 
become rich, by not taxing away the fruits of enterprise in confiscatory 
fashion. 

The family is the most important engine of growth, as parents pass on 
their intellectual capital to their sons and daughters. Government must 
not interfere by rewarding those families that break up, but must 
rather provide incentives for families to hold together. When 
government gives gifts to the poor on the condition that they remain 
poor, this stands in the way of their becoming rich. Success comes 
through failure-repeated failure. In life, as more clearly in sports, 
trying again and again to reach a goal, failing, but failing less and less 
with each attempt, produces achievement. The opportunity to fail is 
the opportunity to succeed. 

Both American political parties have elements of both Jefferson and 
Hamilton. Both have establishment wings and entrepreneurial wings, 
although the entrepreneurial wing of the Democratic Party has been 
moribund since the death of John Kennedy. Presidents Nixon and Ford 
represented the establishment wing of the GOP, President Reagan the 
entrepreneurial wing. President Bush-who is both a Connecticut 
Yankee and a Texas wildcatter-embodies elements of both. In each 
party, the establishment wing concentrates on protecting its turf 
against new competition at home and competition from abroad. The 
entrepreneurial wing is both international and evangelical, wishing to 
spread the message of dynamic, competitive capitalism. 

The work of the populist conservative in the next generation is to 
strengthen the entrepreneurial wings of both parties. Corporate 
socialists have dominated American political thinking for the last half-
century. The promise of the twenty-first century is the promise of 
democratic entrepreneurial capitalism, extending to the inner cities of 
the United States, the developing nations of the Third World, and the 
rebuilding nations of the former Eastern bloc. As President Reagan put 
it in his 1980 acceptance speech in Detroit: "We have got to move 
ahead, but we cannot leave anyone behind." Ill 

Charles Murra 

IT MAKES SENSE to be a little schizophrenic about the American 
future. Much is positive, whether one thinks about the future 
personally (George Gilder's vision of the post-television age sounds 
Eke wonderful fun) or politically (technology is going to give the 
centralized state a very tough time). But there is a dark side looming. 

The dark side flows from a prediction that in itself seems innocuous: 
As national wealth grows in the coming years, so will the proportion of 
people who are rich. I use "rich" roughly, referring generally to 
discretionary income. People who are rich have a lot of it. 

To get an overall idea of the breadth of the trend to date, consider that, 
as of the end of the Korean War, using constant 1988 dollars, less than 
one family in fifty thousand had an income of $ 100,000 or more. By 
1988, almost four families per hundred had an income that great. This 
is a phenomenally large change. The numbers of the rich will tend to 
grow more rapidly in the coming years. Several factors lead to this 
conclusion, principally the increasing monetary value of cognitive 
skills, meaning a combination of ability and training for complex 
mental work. This trend has been in evidence for some time. In 1980, 
for example, a male college graduate made about 30 per cent more 
than a male high-school graduate. By 1988, he made about 60 per cent 
more. In just eight years, the premium for a college degree doubled-in 
comparison with a high school diploma. The comparison with people 
who didn't even graduate from high school is starker yet. 

In coming years, the price for first-rate cognitive skills will skyrocket, 
for reasons involving the nature of changes in technology (constantly 
more complex at the leading edge), polities (constantly creating more 
complicated laws with more complicated loopholes), and the size of 
the stakes (when a percentage point of market share is worth hundreds 
of millions of dollars, then the people who can help you get that extra 
percentage point are worth very large incomes). Meanwhile, real 
wages for low-skill jobs will increase slowly if at all, and efforts to 
increase wages artificially (by raising the minimum wage, for example) 
will backfire because the demand for low-skill labor is becoming more 
elastic as alternatives to human labor become numerous and 
affordable. 

The net result is that the rich are going to constitute a major chunk of 
the population in the relatively near future, and this group will 
increasingly be the most talented. Why be depressed by this prospect, 
which in many ways sounds like a good thing? Because I fear its 
potential for producing something very like a caste society, with the 
implication of utter social separation that goes with that most un-
American of words. 

Briefly, I am trying to envision what happens when 10 or 20 per cent of 
the population has enough income to bypass the social institutions it 
doesn't like in ways that only the top fraction of 1 per cent used to be 
able to do. Robert Reich has called it the "secession of the successful." 
The current symbol of this phenomenon is the gated community. But 
there are many other straws in the wind. A simple example is the way 
that the fax, modem, and Federal Express have already made the U.S. 
Postal Service nearly irrelevant to the way some segments of American 
society communicate. A more portentous example is the mass exodus 
from public schools among urban elites. I sympathize with many of the 
reasons why people with money take these steps. For almost three 
decades now, government has failed miserably to perform its basic 
functions, from preserving order in public spaces to dispensing justice 
to providing decent education in its schools. But the reasonableness of 
the motives does not diminish the danger of the potential 
consequences. 

As this American caste system takes shape, American conservatism is 
going to have to wrestle with its soul. Is conservatism going to follow 
the Latin American model, where to be conservative means to preserve 
the mansions on the hills above the slums? Or is it going to remain true 
to its American heritage, where the thing-to-be-conserved has not been 
primarily money or privilege but a distinctively American way of self-
government and limited government? 

All the forces that I can discern will tend to push American 
conservatism toward the Latin American model. For example: 
Conservatives are now being joined by defectors from urban liberalism 
who have been mugged-sometimes figuratively, often literally. These 
new conservatives are not fans of either Russell Kirk or Milton 
Friedman. Their political agenda is weighted heavily toward taking 
care of number one, using big government to do so whenever it suits 
their purposes. More broadly, the culture of the urban underclass, 
increasingly violent and bizarre, fosters alienation. As each new social 
experiment fails to diminish the size of the underclass, our increasing 
national wealth will make it tempting to bypass the problem by 
treating the inner city as an urban analogue of the Indian reservation. 

This temptation will be augmented by the increasing power of people 
at the upper end of the income scale to use government for their own 
ends. If the rich constitute 10 to 20 per cent of the population, their 
political power will be so immense as to transform the power equation. 
The Left has been complaining for years that the rich have too much 
power. They ain't seen nothing yet. 

It will be sadly ironic if the politics of caste are called "conservative," 
for the greatest bulwarks against the power of privilege are some good 
old-fashioned American conservative principles. Enforce strict equality 
of individuals before the law. Prohibit the state from favoring groups, 
including rich and influential groups. Decentralize government 
authority to the smallest possible unit. None of these principles is a 
panacea, for the forces that will tend to produce an American caste 
system are powerful and complex. But these classic conservative 
principles are more needed than ever, at a time when the seductions 
for conservatives to abandon them are increasing.

